{
  "title": "Ingredient catalog step 2: bulk-classify the pipeline and prod ingredient corpus with Claude Code subagents and commit the result",
  "description": "After step 1 (the shared catalog and resolve() in ramekin-core/src/catalog/, see its README), most written names still won't be in the alias table. \
Grow the committed catalog offline using subagents inside the Claude Code harness (no OpenRouter spend) rather than hand-curating. \
The long tail is too big for hand aliases: in prod alone, aliasing the top 400 unmatched names would cover only ~52% of unmatched lines.\n\
\n\
Corpus, in priority order:\n\
1. \
The make pipeline output. \
Run every pipeline recipe's ingredients through the parser and resolve() to get the full distinct-item set with frequencies. \
It is far larger than prod: data/density-gap-report.txt alone has 6,533 distinct parsed names missing density, and data/unique-ingredients.txt has 16,299 distinct raw lines.\n\
2. \
The prod DB (~/code/ramekin local Postgres; the owner's data, so no PII concern): 3,083 distinct unmatched names as of 2026-09-27, heavily overlapping (1).\n\
3. data/shopping-list-categories.json for non-recipe store items (category only; no nutrition needed).\n\
\n\
Process:\n\
1. \
A make target exports the distinct normalized names still unresolved across those corpora, with combined frequency, which corpora each came from, and 2-3 example raw lines each.\n\
2. \
Shard (e.g. ~200 names per shard, highest frequency first) and fan out subagents. \
Each maps a name to an existing catalog id, proposes a new food entry (USDA FDC id(s) chosen from the pinned snapshot, never invented numbers), proposes a new product entry (purchasable non-food such as a dish soap variant: shopping category only, no FDC id, nutrition, or density), marks it not-a-food, or flags it uncertain. \
Output is structured JSON per shard. \
Since the corpus is large, work in frequency tiers and measure with the audit after each tier; the tail below a frequency cutoff can be left to step 3.\n\
3. \
A validator (a Rust test or make target) checks every proposed food entry's FDC id exists in the snapshot and its kcal/density come from the snapshot, product entries carry a valid shopping category and no nutrition fields, ids are well-formed, and there are no alias conflicts.\n\
4. \
A second verification pass (different subagents) spot-checks a random sample plus every high-calorie mapping (oils, meats, cheese, nuts, sugar), since those dominate estimate error.\n\
5. \
Commit the merged alias/catalog data with a provenance note (date, harness, model), the before/after audit report, and the make pipeline data/ diffs.\n\
\n\
Shopping categories moved to p2-catalog-shopping-categories. \
Progress: tier 1 (top 2,000 names, 2026-09-28) is done; see the 'Classification passes' section of ramekin-core/src/catalog/README.md and docs/agent/catalog-classification.md (make ingredient-catalog-unresolved, the workflow, make catalog-apply-classification). \
Pipeline fixtures now recognize 77.8% of food-line names (from 54.0%), prod 80.1%. \
What limits calories now: 21.8% of lines are still unrecognized names (a long tail: of 13.9k queued names, 9k+ appear once), and 13.1% are recognized foods counted by the piece ('3 eggs', '2 cloves garlic'), which is p2-nutrition-count-units, not this issue. \
Next: decide whether a tier 2 (e.g. names seen 2-3 times) is worth its cost before step 3 takes the tail. \
Success target: >=90% of non-negligible ingredient lines resolved on both the pipeline and prod corpora, and most prod recipes fully estimated. \
Document the procedure in docs/agent/ so it can be rerun as the corpus grows, and so step 3's resolution table can be reviewed and promoted into the committed catalog.",
